[
  {
    "episode_id": 42576,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42576.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 0,
    "turn_text": "Good afternoon, everyone. Today I'm wearing a University of Finlay tie. Fran reminded me as I was leaving today that the University's museum is home to more than 14,000 pieces of artwork from picture books, making it the largest, most diverse collection of picture book art in the world. And I guess some amazing children's art, children's books are there that we have enjoyed seeing. Also want to mention Lydia Mahalek, who is our Director of the Ohio Development Service Agency, is a 2002 graduate, the University of Finley, and was also a member of the Finley Euler Women's Basketball Team. We're opening today with the video that we received from the Greater Dayton Regional Transportation Association. public transportation remains an essential service during these times of crisis. Without it, many of our fellow Ohioans simply could not get to their jobs, to the grocery store, and pharmacy. And that's what they depend on. In Dayton, RTA buses carry 30,000 people each day. For many, RTA is the only option they have to access critical life-sustaining medical appointments, grocery and pharmacy stores. Well, thank the RTA and our other public transit systems in Ohio for all they are doing each and every day. So Eric, let's see the video. This week is National Emergency Medical Services Week. It's a chance to recognize and thank the paramedics, the EMTs, ER doctors and nurses, all those who respond to 911 calls who transport people to our emergency departments for treatment and health crisis situations. These men and women provide the day-to-day life-saving services of medicine's front line. They are brave. They are true professionals who dedicate their lives to helping others. So thank you to each and every one of you for what you do. We appreciate you this week, and we appreciate you every single day. Thanks for your great work. We continue to get some inquiries. I got some this weekend about visitation policies at hospitals. And I know that during this coronavirus, it's difficult. There's some difficult decisions that are made about. Okay. Okay. I guess the test work. Good to know. Whatever. But getting back to the hospital rules, we know that these are just tough situations today when hospitals are trying to deal with the COVID-19 and stop the spread at the same time. loved ones, want to see loved ones. We have no rules. The State of Ohio has not issued any rule. Dr. Acton has not issued any rule in regard to visitation. We have not done that throughout this COVID-19. So these rules are being set by the individual hospitals. So I want to make sure that people understand that we are not setting those rules for the hospitals. They are making these decisions based on what they think is the best as far as from a medical point of view. But we have nothing to do with those rules. Last week we talked about that we would be focusing on the work of our minority health, health strike force. And I want to thank the members of that strike force who are working. My team and I have had several conversations this weekend about the strike force and recommendations and really about our obligation in Ohio. to protect our most vulnerable members of society, our most vulnerable populations from COVID-19. on Thursday of this week. I will be talking more in depth about what we are doing with that task force, what some of the recommendations are, some of the things that we have already done, and some things that we intend to do in the future. So we will be doing that on Thursday. Again, if you look at the numbers, as Dr. Acton has pointed out, the death rate from COVID-19, for example, among African Americans, is stunningly high. So we will be looking at that on Thursday and talking about what we have done and what we intend to do. Yesterday, the Ohio Department of Mental Health and Addiction Services received notice that one of their patients at Twin Valley Behavioral Health Hospital in Columbus who had symptoms of COVID-19 did, in fact, test positive for the virus. Later, two other patients in the same unit got back positive results. In response to the COVID to these positive tests, the Department notified the Ohio Department of Health and the Ohio State University for consultation. Ohio State is working with the Department of Mental Health and Addiction Services to arrange for testing of all the other patients in that 25-person unit. Testing will be conducted in an on-site designated area. Patients in the unit are currently quarantined. And those who test positive are being isolated to prevent the spread of COVID-19. All patients in the hospital are screened multiple times a day and staff members are screened at the beginning of their shifts. This will of course continue. All impacted staff have been notified. and are being actively monitored for symptoms and are being urged to be tested. In addition, members working in this unit are now using full PPE including mass, gowns, gloves, shoe covers and face shields. The hospital has stopped taking new civil admissions and is working with alcohol, drug and mental health boards in impacted counties and local psychiatric hospitals in Franklin County to divert patients from there. The Department of Mental Health and Addiction Services has been monitoring development since the beginning of the COVID-19 crisis and has taken action to prevent the spread of the disease in their hospitals. The Department will continue to work with the Ohio Department of Health and Ohio State University to try and keep patients and employees safe and healthy. Updates on the COVID-19 situation at the state's behavioral health hospitals are made every afternoon, and you can check on those. They can be found on the coronavirus website at coronavirus.ohile.gov, coronavirus.ohile.gov. And those are posted every single day. I want to talk a little bit about restaurants and bars. I've always tried to be blunt, straightforward about everything that we are finding. And we try to share with you all the information that we have. I have been very candid in saying that in this reopening phase, a new chapter in the COVID-19 There is risk involved. There are significant risk. But there are also significant risk if we do not open up. And we've talked about those as well. So we can do two things at once. And it's up really to all of us to make sure that we open our economy, get people back to work, but do this in an absolutely safe way. The protocols, the rules for reopening bars and restaurants were laid down very, very clearly. by the working group that we put together made up of health officials, but also made up of people who run bars and people who run restaurants. The truth is that our economic recovery, our economic recovery in the state of Ohio, is tied directly to how successful we are in preventing the spread of the coronavirus. Stopping the spread, interrupting the spread of the coronavirus will determine how successful we are in opening up our economy. These two are tied together. You cannot have a successful reopening of the economy if we get surges in the coronavirus. And so these two are as linked together or as anything can be linked together. We are talking about the slowing of the spread and the basic facts have not changed. That can only be done by distancing. spread of the disease distance. The more distance, the less spread of the disease. There certainly is an inverse relationship between the two. Very, very important. And so as we look at how our restaurants operate and how our bars operate, the distance is absolutely the key thing. As we got reports and we're still getting them from health departments all over the state. But as we got reports over the weekend, it was clear that most restaurants were doing an amazingly good job. Most bars were doing a good job. But it's clear that we have had some outliers, ones who just were not doing what they should do. If we're going to make this recovery, if we're going to bring the economy back, people have to feel safe when they go out. They have to feel that the rules are being followed when they go to a restaurant. They have to feel the rules are being followed when they go to a bar. So let me read from the order that Dr. Acton signed. And again, that was based upon recommendations from Let me pull this up. This is based on recommendations from the working group. I'm just going to read part of it. Restaurants and bars to reopen. All restaurants, bars, and other like businesses and operations in the state, which have the on-site consumption of food, beer, wine, liquor, permitted to reopen for full dining service within the state, so long as all workplace safety standards are met. These businesses and operations are encouraged to either reopen or remain open if they have not ceased operation during the prior stay-at-home order. Business and operations shall continue and here we get to the key part. Business and operations shall continue to comply with the social distancing requirements as defined in this order, including by maintaining six-foot social distancing for both employees and for members of the public at all times, including but not limited to when any customers are standing in line. Reservations are to be limited to more than 10 persons. Customers, and here is a key part, particularly for the bars. Customers must be seated when consuming food, beer, wine, and liquor on the premises of the business. applies to restaurants, applies to bars. Customers must be seated when consuming food, beer, wine, and liquor on the premises of the business. And it continues on, but that is a key part. So what we saw in some bars this weekend with pictures, some pictures that made national news, we saw were in some places, customers were not following the rules, and the people who were running the bar were not taking responsibility. So both have to do that. The owner of the bar The people who are running the bar, they have an obligation to control the environment. And if they cannot control the environment, they should make the wise judgment not to open. Or if they get in a situation where they can't control it, they need to close. I want to talk about enforcement. And again, I want to make it very clear. At least as far as we could tell. The majority of restaurants were doing a great job. Most of the bars were doing a decent job. But there were some outliers. And this jeopardizes, frankly, our ability to move forward as a state. So I've asked our team to come up with a plan, and they have the following plan. We are marshalling all the resources at our disposal to assemble a large contingent of law enforcement and health officials from across state agencies and from our local communities. We will coordinate with them as part of the Department of Public Safety's Ohio investigative unit known as OIU and the work that they do. So we're going to bring in people from different departments and put them into that unit. So they will surge in to conduct safety compliance checks in crowded bars and restaurants. They will issue, they will issue administrative citations that can result in the revocation of liquor licenses further. They will work with municipal prosecutors to take potential criminal action against these bad actors. So that is what they will be doing. We will be working very closely with local officials on this. The local health departments will stay involved as well. Ohio Department of Veterans Service operates two nursing homes for veterans in Ohio, one in Sandusky and the other in Georgetown. Due to the department's proactive work, which really began in February to keep the virus out of these homes, we've had no positive cases of COVID-19 among residents until just recently. Now one staff member at the Sandeschi home tested positive and that was actually in mid-April and did not what returned to work. But then on Thursday of last week, four residents at that facility tested positive. So we are committed to doing absolutely everything we can to protect each and every veteran in these homes. These men and women served our country. They protected us and we are committed to protecting them. Last week I directed veteran service director Deborah Ashenhurst to arrange for the testing of each and every resident and staff member in both homes in an effort to limit the spread. The rapid mobilization to get this testing done right away frankly was remarkable. The local health departments and local medical facilities stepped up and were very, very helpful as did our team at the Ohio Department of Health. In Georgetown, members of the Ohio National Guard responded over the weekend to take the testing samples in the Ohio State Highway Patrol, transported the test to and from the facility. As of today, nearly everyone in both nursing homes have been tested. That's more than 700 staff members and more than 500 nursing home residents. We are also providing testing to Sandusky's independent living facility for veterans. That testing was completed this morning. While we're still waiting test results for around 50% of the cases, about 50% of the individuals, this is where we are as of today. There are still zero positive cases in the Georgetown facility. In Sandusky, we now have 23 confirmed residents and three confirmed staff. A total of 50 and eight staff in residents have been tested negative so far. of the residents who tested positive. They're receiving around-the-clock care in the home's enhanced care facility and at Firelunge Regional Medical Center, which is Sandusky's partner hospital. As test results continue to be returned, our Ohio Veterans home will keep any Veterans testing positive in the enhanced care unit so they're separated from the others and so their care can be closely monitored. Also, the Veterans' Homes have enhanced their internal communication so that residents, staff, and family members receive immediate information. They have also begun posting updated data about testing in both facilities on our website coronavirus.ohile.gov. I would like now to introduce you to another Ohio one who has successfully battled COVID-19. Stacey from Northeast Ohio, her battle was certainly not an easy one and she'll tell you about it. She wants to tell you her story in hopes that those who hear it will do all they can to prevent this from happening to other Ohio citizens. Stacey was in the hospital for weeks. She spent days on a vent later in a medically induced coma. Her family believed she was going to die and she almost did. Stacy, thank you for joining us. We are very, very grateful and we invite you to just kind of tell us a little bit about your story.",
