    "episode_id": 42576,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42576.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "Hi, thank you. I just want to commend the swift shutting down of Ohio. I feel that because of those actions, it contributed to saving my life. I put myself in a self-quarantine two weeks before I knew anything was going to happen with the state of Ohio. And I still ended up getting COVID-19. I was exposed on March 8th. And by March 21st, I was in Geneva emergency room with double lobe pneumonia. They admitted me. and within 24 hours, they were intubating me and shipping me downtown. And I don't remember anything else from there. I had an appointment today with Dr. Gold, a surgeon at Geneva Hospital, and he was instrumental in saving my life. He made a profound comment today that had There have been 10 to 20 more patients flooded with what I had. I probably wouldn't have made it. So he said that he will never forget me. And I'm grateful that he saved my life. I'm grateful that they innovated me and got me on the ventilator. And the one other thing that he said as far as my life being saved downtown, at University Hospital was that I was in a prone position, that they were able to do that for me. The care was incredible. I'm just grateful for the way that your cabinet has handled everything that Dr. Acton. And I can't stress enough to people. When this first happened to me, I said to my niece, This is a very serious thing and people need to know it. And I want to be able to be a voice and tell people, whether or not you have a suppressed system or not, we all need to be mindful of each other and ourselves to wear the masks and to wear them responsibly. If we know we're going to be in public and we know we have a compromised immune system, then wear the mask. Or don't go out if you're concerned or afraid. I just can't stress enough that I just wish people would be more mindful that this is really something real. My two months flat on my back and being paralyzed were something I'll never forget. Getting better. And I'm walking a little bit in the house without a walker. And I am going to get better. And I know that I will.",
