  {
    "episode_id": 42576,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42576.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "We did have another staff person pass away, as you mentioned, then nurse Bernard will be missed by his family and his correctional family, but also by the patients that he faithfully treated and served. He was one of 12,000 staff that come to work every day, even though they know there's COVID all over the prison. And they do their job and they take care of the people that are there and they care. We've had three staff members pass away from COVID as well as 61 incarcerated adults. And we feel every single one of those. We work every day to try to not have another. This is a vicious disease and not everyone is making it through. We don't work alone. We get help from specialists, from environmental health specialists to epidemiologists, infectious disease specialists, physicians, hospitals, all to try to do the very best we can for our patients. We follow the CDC guidelines for prisons and have them implemented everywhere. And we QA those to make sure that we have quality assurance and that things are happening everywhere. We have 2,100 fewer people living in our system than we did when this started or as of March 24th. And, you know, that helps us spread people apart farther. As you mentioned, social distancing is one of the very important things in terms of trying to control the spread of this disease. And that's the one luxury that we really don't have in the Department of Rehabilitation and Correction. We have more space, but we don't have huge amounts of space. We did start testing our patients early on with signs and symptoms as was recommended by the health department at the time. And those recommendations have evolved over time with availability of testing and with additional science. The next evolution we took was to mass test, and we did that at three facilities. We thought we would find asymptomatic people a 20%, maybe 30%, that we could move elsewhere and sort of quarantine off so that everyone else could be safe. But we found that testing is really just a point in time It doesn't really tell you about people who have incubated and they don't have enough viral load to make a positive. And it doesn't really help you if you find out that 96% of your positive people are asymptomatic and positive rather than 20 to 30% you thought you were going to find. So once we really found that kind of spread, you know, something that Dr. Acton said and taught us early on is certainly happening inside the prisons. And that is, she said repeatedly early on we may get to a point. where community spread is so vast that testing is no longer recommended. And while thankfully we did not get into that situation in the state of Ohio, we have gotten into that situation in the Department of Rehabilitation and Correction. So with all, armed with all this knowledge, what should we be doing? Well, we should be treating people. And that's what we're doing. We're looking for signs and symptoms. We're treating people. We have a new testing plan that we're following. And it's based upon, you know, interacting with the experts as well as utilizing the science of what we found going on inside prisons because we were the first people to mass test. We're the first people to get results back. And now we're going to be the first people to evolve because we need to do things that help us take action to help people and save people. We are using this new testing plan at Belmont Correctional Institution. I think over 200 people have been tested there already. Some of the elements of this new testing plan involve testing people on the way into our system and then quarantining them for 14 days and not allowing them to really leave reception for 35 days. This process has revealed a female that was received at the High Reformatory for Women Reception Center. And she was tested positive upon injury. And so therefore we're now testing the people that she lived with to make sure that it hasn't spread to them. Now we have to wait an appropriate number of days so that we're not testing so soon after her arrival that it didn't have time to incubate and grow in someone else's system. Once that time period has passed, we will be testing them and they're in quarantine now.",
