  {
    "episode_id": 42576,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42576.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 36,
    "turn_text": "unit. He may get enough testing to determine that he thinks it's pretty much spread everywhere and that he would cease testing and just begin treating. So it's based upon his clinical decision-making. We also test people pre-surgery and we test people on the way out. If you are leaving early, we will keep retesting you until you're negative and then you'll leave. And if you're leaving on time, we would test you and keep you quarantined after testing. And then if you are positive, we would give you a letter that's based on information from Dr. Acton's order. that would allow the community and local health department to work with you and make sure you're quarantined the proper amount of time before you begin to mix with other people. I say all that to say that we are testing when it is actionable, testing when it helps someone, testing when someone can be either quarantined safely away, not put in contact with someone else or when it's their healthcare is gonna be impacted. So that's kind of how our testing system works. We also screen our population. So at a place like Belmont Correctional Institution, where there are positive people, we would screen every single inmate there for symptomology and temperature. And also we would use a pulse oximeter to make sure that they still have adequate oxygen saturation. At this point, we've tested about 17% of our entire population in the department. And we continue to test wherever we think it's clinically appropriate or where we can impact things. The next evolution we're going to be making is to utilize antibody testing. And we've already done that at least three facilities. And that helps us further understand what's going on in a prison. The totality of this helps us make sure that we can operate as safely as possible for our staff and for our inmates. Marion was our hotspot at one point and they've come out the other side. They have like 1,600 people that have recovered and that's, that's great. That's what we like to see. That's obviously the goal. But our hotspot now is Belmont Correctional Institution. So we've done some things like Surgeon the National Guard. We've put SRT members and star members in there from other prisons. We also are utilizing the Highway Patrol to make sure that we can staff while the people who work there that are ill have time to recover. We are using multiple hospitals, multiple ambulance contracts. We've brought in things like oxygen concentrators and mobile showers so that we can use non-traditional housing and segregate people that are ill or put them in places where the staff can easily care for them. this is not easy what we're doing and the people at Belmont Correctional Institution aren't there a team and they're really doing great work. I know there's a lot of COVID there now but for weeks that facility was able to keep it to just one unit and that may not sound like much but when you're talking about COVID and as the the woman before me said if you have everyone show up sick at the same time you can't handle that. And Beaumont's done an excellent job of keeping it contained to one unit for a lengthy period of time, which allows that unit to be in recovery. And now, yes, it's going to other places, but it's not all happening at once. That's about the staff doing the right things every day. We can't stop it. It's open-loving. There is no real social distancing. We've got people six feet apart now, but that's not adequate when you live there all day, every day, right? If it wasn't for the efforts of that staff and the teamwork of that staff, everyone would have had COVID at the same time and it would have overwhelmed the community and that's not what has happened. We really will continue to fight every day, but just understand that a prison system is not a place when it's open bay living where you can keep COVID out for any length of time and it's not a place where it's not going to spread. So we have to deal with that as best we can every day and that's what we're doing.",
