  {
    "episode_id": 42576,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42576.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 46,
    "turn_text": "Thank you Governor. Good afternoon. I have three items to cover today. The first may be somewhat redundant. But I feel it's important. I've spent the good part of the last month reviewing the work of our various work groups, including the one that I want to talk about today, which is the work group on what restaurants and bars and the compliance issues that arose over the weekend. Remember, outdoor dining started on Friday. Indoor will start on the 21st. And we have that work group did a great job at trying to put protocols in place that could be followed, that people could comply with. They tested them with all different types of owners. These rules must be followed not for our benefit, not for the director, the governor, or mine. They must be followed for the benefit of all, benefit of the public, you. the people who are watching this news conference and everybody else out there who you love and care for. It's for the employees to keep them safe. And it's for the customers who want to help a business owner, want to go enjoy a meal and perhaps a drink. But we need to do it safely. And that's why those protocols were put in place. And I want to be clear. This is, today is again an education process on this. Patrons must be seated. They're required to be seated. There must be a seat for everybody who's consuming food or drinks. And open congregate areas must remain closed. There's no more than 10 people to a party to a table and all those tables must be six feet apart or separated by a barrier. And if you can't do this as a business owner because you're accountable for this. It's not your patrons that are accountable and yes we expect them to be but ultimately you're accountable for this and if you cannot do it then you cannot open. And this process that we continue to go through is to educate you because we want you to comply. We don't want anybody to be out of compliance. because we don't want to enforce it. Enforcement's the last straw essentially. of us want to get to that. But if you can't comply, and if you won't comply, And if you won't keep people safe, your customers and your employees, then there has to be enforcement. It's the only way this works for us to keep people safe. And also know that local officials can go above and beyond what the state standard is in this. So if there are particular problems, say, in an entertainment district or in a community, local officials can set stricter standards and even close those types of facilities for operation. So I want to be clear about that and help to educate folks so we get this right. We are all in this together. Nobody wants to see a couple of folks ruin the situation for all because it is the governor mentioned we got to keep people safe. Each other safe so that we don't have the spread so we can get the economy moving again. We can build confidence and keep people healthy. Second item that I'd like to discuss is around sports, sports leagues, gyms, fitness centers, which we discussed last week, opening up on May the 26th, particularly sports that are non-contact or low-contact like tennis paddle sports, golf baseball and softball. And I mentioned we are working on standards for other sports that are contact or low-contact, at least for the training portion of those. In that order and in that discussion we had last week, it did not address, it did not involve school, your school related sporting activities. Well, this morning I spoke with Jerry Snodgrass, who's the executive director at the Ohio High School Athletic Association. We are going to begin to coordinate with them on protocols for training and preparation for school sanctions sports. We know that this school year is nearing the end. But for the athletes out there, we know that training is a year-round process. We know that properly trained, properly fit folks. It's an important thing, not just to excel, but to lower the risk of injuries and things like that. We don't know what the school and fall will hold at this point in time. But we do know, like with gyms and fitness centers, that non-contact sporting activities, And the training for even those that are contact, that there are many ways that you can do that that are safe. And we know that you want to keep that future in mind. Everybody wants to have something to hope for. Keeping ourselves fit and trained is important. We know the schools and parents and the student athletes are looking to prepare for that future. And we will work with the Ohio High School Athletic Association to develop protocols. to make sure that we limit the ability to spread COVID-19. That's the goal of everything we do is to limit the spread to create, keep people safe, so we will begin the process of working with them on what that might look like. More information to come on this conversation in the near future, but wanted to clarify that. And then finally, on churches, it always seems to pop up When are you going to open churches? Churches have never been closed under the director's order and the governor's supervision of that. Churches have not been closed. Many churches have chosen to close because of their responsibility to keep their parishioners safe, and some of them have opened following appropriate guidelines. But remember, the distancing, the disinfecting, the masks, all of those things help us keep each other safe. It's important that we all make sure that no matter what we're doing, as we're going back out in our workplace and our personal lives, that we know what works to control the spread and that we are holding each other accountable in that and that we are looking, I guess since we're talking about churches to be our brother's keeper to love our neighbor as ourself and to try to make sure that we're responsible in these activities. Governor?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_12",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
