    "turn_idx": 68,
    "turn_text": "And again, I have not talked specifically with the director about this particular case, but I know she's been consulting with our medical team as well as other clinicians. And so I think it's really important to remember that the testing is not the treatment and you want the testing to guide your contact tracing, especially early on when you can test and you can find someone and you can remove people from the situation and spread it out. You can actually use it as part of your strategy and it's very, very, that's why we really, in our tiers of testing, ask that a clinician in health departments actually go into a facility and look at the layout because every story is a little different in the disease detective work. Now these tests, as we know, many people are asymptomatic, but also many people are too early in their disease process. So if you test on one day, it's actually one point in time to really catch people. You'd almost have to do it every three days. And so when you're talking about mass testing, the big question is always like, what do you use that for? Will it change how you do your treatment? So for instance, something people don't know unless you've been tested. My husband was sick. I had the good or bad fortune myself of being tested because I wanted to make sure he wasn't tested and then I was tested. And if you have had this test, you know that it's not a simple little test you do. It's fairly invasive. It's not necessarily comfortable having the swab put down. So it has to be done by medical professionals in a very distinct way. something like the finger prick that we're hoping for as the answer where we could just take a little drop of blood and test people. So that's something you also really have to weigh. So there are many factors that go into testing. There is no limit, as the governor said, he will never limit in any of our congregate care settings, the ability to do that. But what we're learning around the country now is how do we do that well and smartly and take the patient? and the staff into consideration as well. So I think it's become a more complicated equation than just our wish for, if we could just test everyone, we would know everything we need to know.",
