    "turn_text": "in, let me just add to that, that, you know, for example, in some prisons we've had 80% positive and, you know, the question is, you know, you don't, at some point you no longer have the ability to isolate people. So, again, I want to assure everyone that the contact tracing is being done. When someone, let's say in Belmont or any other prison, is test positive, or let's say there is a member of the staff that tests positive. That contact tracing is done. Let's say it's a member of the staff. It's a contract. Contact tracing is done inside the prison where they see who that person is coming contact with, but they also do it outside the prison and outside the prison is done by the local health department. So it's the same basic principle that is used everywhere, that if you have sufficient testing, you do the testing, you do the tracing, you isolate, and you find out who that person has actually been in contact with. The difficulty when you're dealing with the prisoners when he has spread throughout the prison comes back to what do you do with that information? Because the treatment is basically the same as my understanding. Is that right, Dr. Atkins? The treatment",
