    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42576.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 78,
    "turn_text": "You know, I think I'll share this with you. You know, we know congregate settings are very high risk. And whether they would be a workplace where people spend eight hours together, whether it's our home, any place that we're in close proximity is going to remain a difficulty as we move forward. And that's why we have to take all the precautions we're taking. I want to say something about this. You know, I failed to mention this earlier, to been on my mind, but health care. I really need people to get out there, not be afraid of going to hospitals right now and seeking health care. I know many of us delayed care. I will share with you that it's very difficult, this whole idea of visitors. And it's something that we have teams of people looking at very carefully. We're talking to experts all over the country to understand that, you know, the spread of disease, especially in nursing homes where people go from nursing home to nursing home to nursing home, which would not be unusual as a physician maybe. I would visit several nursing homes. I then can be like transmitting that. And we saw that. We saw that early out in Washington where we really learned about this virus. We saw it here in Ohio. And so at the same time, I just want to say, this visitation thing is something that we need to keep looking at. We're looking at how can we have more PPE. PPE shortage has been a factor in all of this. Now we're learning more about using masks. So these are policies we'll keep revisiting. My very own daughter had to use the healthcare system. She has a very bad tonsillators and was hospitalized and is hospitalized right now. So, Maddie, I love you. I know you're there and it's very hard as a mom to not go in the emergency room with her. She's 23. We will say that. She's an adult. But we all know that we like to have our advocates with us and people. And so I just want to say to people out there, thank you to our healthcare professionals. Thank you to our nursing home people. who are taking such loving care of the people we love, because this is probably one of the hardest things that I've heard from every friend with every parent in a nursing home. And this will be something we continue to look at as we move forward.",
